			<div class="content" id="post-content-4736811">
									

<p>I have to agree with the sentiments expressed here. The references feel unnecessary to me for the most part, and don't go further into any kind of connection that would deepen the intrigue of the story beyond &quot;this is a thing.&quot;</p>
<p>Additionally, while I did quite enjoy the logs at first, the sheer number of them and the more or less redundancy of their message made them become a bit of a drag over time. There's very little sense of rising tension across them, making some of the later entries a bit harder to read because they communicate the same message that we've already received: Goya is losing his mind.</p>
<p>Ultimately though, the central premise is very good, and the writing is solid. I just think that this could probably be pared down a bit and better focused.</p>

